<Guide
  slug="first-run"
  title="First run"
  description="Create your vault, choose a passphrase, and save the recovery code that is shown only once."
  lede="Setting up takes about a minute. One step of it cannot be undone later, so it is worth understanding before you click through it."
>
  <h2>What happens when you open Sanctum</h2>
  <p>
    There is no account to create, no email to confirm, and nothing sent anywhere. The first
    window asks you to choose a passphrase, and that passphrase is what creates the vault. Until
    you set one, there is no vault file on disk at all.
  </p>

  <Steps steps={[
    'Run the installer and open Sanctum. Windows will warn you about the unsigned installer &mdash; the <a href="../../download/">download page</a> explains why and what to do.',
    'Type a passphrase, twice. Sanctum shows a live strength meter as you type.',
    'Wait a moment. On first launch only, Sanctum measures how fast your machine is and tunes its key derivation to match.',
    '<strong>Write down the recovery code.</strong> Six groups of five characters. It is displayed once and never again.',
    'Confirm you have saved it. The vault opens, empty.',
  ]} />

  <h2>Choosing a passphrase</h2>
  <p>
    Sanctum requires a strength score of at least 3 out of 4 <em>and</em> a minimum of twelve
    characters. Both, not either. The strength score comes from zxcvbn, which estimates how many
    guesses an attacker would need &mdash; it knows about common passwords, keyboard patterns,
    dates, and names, so <code>P@ssw0rd123!</code> scores badly despite looking complicated.
  </p>
  <p>
    Four unrelated words beat a short scramble on both counts, and you will be typing this twice a
    day. Pick something you can type without looking.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="note" title="Why the pause on first launch">
    <p>
      Sanctum stretches your passphrase with Argon2id, which is deliberately slow and
      memory-hungry so that guessing it in bulk is expensive. How slow depends on your hardware,
      so rather than picking a fixed setting, Sanctum measures your machine and calibrates to
      about three quarters of a second per unlock.
    </p>
    <p>
      A slower laptop gets gentler settings so unlocking stays usable. A faster desktop gets
      harder settings for free. The parameters are stored in the vault header, so your vault
      always opens with the settings it was created with &mdash; even on a different machine.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The recovery code</h2>
  <p>
    Your passphrase does not unlock your records directly. It unwraps a key, and that key decrypts
    your data. The recovery code unwraps a second copy of that same key.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two things follow from that. It can let you back in after a forgotten passphrase &mdash; that
    is its whole purpose. And it is exactly as sensitive as the passphrase itself, because it
    opens exactly the same door.
  </p>

  <Callout kind="warn" title="Store it somewhere else">
    <p>
      A recovery code saved on the same machine as the vault protects you from forgetting, and
      from nothing else. A stolen laptop takes both.
    </p>
    <p>
      A password manager you already trust, a printed copy in a drawer, or a safe are all fine. A
      note on the desktop is not.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>If you lose both</h2>
  <p>
    The data is gone. There is no reset link, no support address that can help, and no backdoor.
    The key is derived from your passphrase and nothing else stores it, which is precisely what
    makes the vault worth using &mdash; and precisely what makes this irreversible.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical answer is a backup, covered in <a href="../backups/">the last guide</a>: an
    encrypted file with its own password, kept somewhere else. If the passphrase goes, that file
    and its password are what remain.
  </p>

  <h2>What is set up for you</h2>
  <ul class="plainList">
    <li><strong>Auto-lock after five minutes idle.</strong> The key is dropped from memory, not just hidden. Adjustable in Settings.</li>
    <li><strong>Website icons off.</strong> Fetching a site's favicon would tell that site you hold a credential for it. Off by default; yours to turn on.</li>
    <li><strong>Clipboard protection on.</strong> Copies are excluded from Windows clipboard history and cloud sync, and cleared after thirty seconds.</li>
  </ul>
</Guide>
